results.answer:
The evaluation found that nurse-led discharge planning:

- reduced mean length of stay from **7.9 days** to **7.1 days**, an adjusted reduction of **0.8 days** (**95% CI 0.5 to 1.1**);
- did **not** increase 30-day emergency readmissions or 30-day deaths;
- increased the proportion of patients discharged before noon from **18.6%** to **29.3%**;
- improved readiness for discharge scores from **6.8** to **7.4** out of 10;
- reduced bed-days lost to delayed discharge from **1,184** to **902**;
- had a larger effect in patients aged **80 and over**: length of stay fell from **10.4** to **9.0 days**.

The board briefing summarises this as the average hospital stay being **1.2 days shorter**, but the evaluation report itself reports the adjusted reduction as **0.8 days**.

ANSWER: Nurse-led discharge planning shortened hospital stays, improved discharge timing and patient readiness, reduced delayed discharge bed-days, and did not increase readmissions or deaths; the evaluation report reports an adjusted mean length-of-stay reduction of **0.8 days**.
